      "key": "discovery_composition_conformance",
      "statement": "Directory nesting creates no inheritance, ownership, composition or override. Every composition entry supplies product_definition, target_definition_id matching target product.definition_id, a relation authority defining direction/roles/owner/scope/lifecycle/invalidation and at least one exact interface/capability/identity/lifecycle/data/evidence/other governing contract. Moving a definition requires a locator update, not identity change; another identity at the same locator fails closed. Exact immutable Product/release identities, when bound, must also be carried and verified by relation/contracts; mutable definition identity cannot substitute. A conforming set requires every definition to validate against its selected released schema; every required URI/fragment to resolve within declared boundaries; globally unique definition IDs and tenant IDs unique within each overlay; applicable resolvable frame bases for every scope and authoritative reconciliation of overlaps; each disambiguation to bind exact term, context, complete material candidate set, selected concept, authority, basis and scope, with resolves_to in disambiguates and a congruent resolution carrier; every material cross-context seam to use explicit import/disambiguation/translation/equivalence, never name matching; every target, relation and non-empty contract set to resolve; and no competing carriers for Product, tenant, ticket-state or constitutional authority. Draft 2020-12 validation establishes structure only; URI formats are annotations unless asserted. Conformance explicitly checks RFC 3986 syntax using an assertion-capable validator, then separately checks resolution, fragments, selected-release identity, cross-file uniqueness, constitutional sufficiency, semantic and cross-context completeness, and authority congruence.",
